  "difficulty_rating": 1600,
  "explanation": "The lambda captures the variable `factor` itself, not its value at the time the lambda was created. Python closures are late-binding, so all three lambdas look up `factor` when they're finally called in the list comprehension — by then the loop that created them has finished and `factor` holds its last value, 3. apply_all(10) therefore returns [30, 30, 30] instead of the intended [10, 20, 30].",
  "prompt": "make_multipliers is meant to hand back three lambdas that each multiply by a different factor (1, 2, 3). Step through apply_all(10) and predict what it returns.",
  "language": "python",
  "snippet": "def make_multipliers():\n    fns = []\n    for factor in range(1, 4):\n        fns.append(lambda x: x * factor)\n    return fns\n\ndef apply_all(value):\n    fns = make_multipliers()\n    return [fn(value) for fn in fns]\n\nprint(apply_all(10))",
